  "analytic_obligations_carried_from_680": [
    "The two sublogarithmic profiles have positive first and second log-radius derivatives; flattening keeps both signs and the fixed cutoff annulus.",
    "The smooth flattened profiles decrease canonically and in L1; the singular center value is really minus infinity.",
    "One positive coefficient controls both annular lower Hessian bounds; the sum of the curvature losses is at most h/4.",
    "Finite-atlas radial L1 bounds and summable coefficients prevent either countable sum from being identically minus infinity.",
    "Every prescribed point remains a pole of a nonpositive summand; dense prescribed poles need not be the complete pole set.",
    "The same stronger sum dominates every finite stretch; common poles are handled by inequalities, not subtraction.",
    "Positive h supplies convexity with zero; each allowed ordinary-order constant is finite but need not be uniform in the stretch.",
    "Weighted Jensen uses the missing tail weight at zero; every exponential integral has a uniform bound after coordinate rescaling.",
    "The radial substitution gives exponent -n*x+s*S*(1+x)^sigma; sublinear growth proves integrability for every real positive s.",
    "Every holomorphic germ is bounded on a smaller ball, proving trivial multiplier ideals in the exp(-s*u) convention.",
    "The internal envelope construction freezes the nonclosed background coefficients and retains its exact lower current bound.",
    "Constants bound the unrestricted continuous-obstacle envelope above and below; continuity retains its pointwise obstacle bound.",
    "Under the book definition the bounded unrestricted envelope has the same trivial ideals as u and is an actual ideal competitor.",
    "Convex obstacle approximants stay below F and satisfy ordinary order through the one fixed stronger sum.",
    "Almost-everywhere convergence off the finite-volume pole set and canonical quasi-psh equality give the everywhere envelope identity.",
    "No Monge--Ampere mass or geometry follows from exponential integrability; the unrestricted S09 implication is not proved."
  ],
